    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Beijing began as the walled city of Ji, a frontier seat on the northern edge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> proper, and was a provincial town for about a thousand years.</li><li>It became a capital under the Jin in 1153, the Mongol Yuan from 1271 and the Ming from 1421, which gave it the plan and monuments it still has.</li><li>It was named Ji, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/youzhou">Youzhou</a>, Nanjing, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhongdu">Zhongdu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadu">Dadu</a>, Beiping and Beijing, and its name and standing changed again in 1928, 1937 and 1949.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Beijing, the Northern Capital, took its name in 1403 to distinguish it from Nanjing, the Southern Capital. It lost the name in 1928, regained it under Japanese occupation in 1937, lost it again in 1945 and recovered it in 1949.</p></aside>
    </section>

