    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1045 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Zhou confer the city states of Ji and Yan, the date being the Beijing Government's official estimate</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">938</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> rename <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/youzhou">Youzhou</a> Nanjing and make it a secondary capital</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1153</span><span class="rf-text">The Jin move their capital to Yanjing and name it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhongdu">Zhongdu</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1271</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> founds the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> and names his capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadu">Dadu</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1403</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a> renames Beiping as Beijing</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1644</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a> take the city and make it the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1900</span><span class="rf-text">The Eight-Nation Alliance lifts the siege of the Legation Quarter and occupies the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1949</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> proclaims the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiananmen">Tiananmen</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/homo-erectus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/homo-erectus.jpg" alt="Homo erectus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/homo-erectus">Homo erectus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homo_erectus_tautavelensis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Luna04, CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The earliest traces of habitation in the Beijing municipality are in the caves of Dragon Bone Hill near Zhoukoudian, where Peking Man, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homo-erectus">Homo erectus</a>, lived; Homo sapiens used the caves from about 27,000 to 10,000 years ago. Over 40 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> settlements and burial sites show that farming was widespread in the area 6,000 to 7,000 years ago.</p><p>The earliest events of the city's history are legend. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a>, the Yellow Emperor defeated the Yan Emperor at Banquan in the 26th century BCE, possibly near Yanqing, and the sage-king Yao is said to have founded a town called Youdu in the region about 4,000 years ago. You, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/youzhou">Youzhou</a>, later became one of the city's names.</p><p>The first event with archaeological support belongs to the 11th century BCE, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a> absorbed the Shang. According to Sima Qian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-wu-of-zhou">King Wu of Zhou</a> conferred titles on the rulers of the city states of Ji and Yan, and the Beijing Government uses 1045 BCE as the official estimate of the date. The seat of Ji, Jicheng, is believed to have stood in the south-west of present urban Beijing, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-people">Guang</a>'anmen, beside a steady supply of water from the Lotus Pool. Yan had its capital at Dongjialin, about 45 km to the south, where a large walled settlement and over 200 tombs have been found. Both lay on a trade route along the eastern flank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taihang-mountains">Taihang Mountains</a>. Some time in the Western Zhou or early Eastern Zhou, Yan conquered Ji and moved its capital there.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Beijing&#8221; cites where it covers Early history, Early and Mid-Imperial China:</p><ul><li>Stephen G. Haw, Beijing – A Concise History (Routledge, 2006)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lugou-bridge"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lugou-bridge.jpg" alt="Lugou Bridge" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lugou-bridge">Lugou Bridge</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lugouqiao-2014.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alanba42, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For about a thousand years after the First Emperor, the city was a provincial seat on the northern edge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> proper. In the early Western Han the four counties of the Principality of Guangyang had 20,740 households and an estimated population of 70,685; in 57 CE the five counties of Guangyang <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">Commandery</a> had 44,550 households and an estimated 280,600 residents. In 742 the ten counties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/youzhou">Youzhou</a> had 67,242 households and 371,312 residents.</p><p>The city's stature grew from the 10th century. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a>, the population inside the walled city rose from 22,000 in 938 to 150,000 in 1113. After the Jin made it their Central Capital in 1153 and doubled its size, it grew from 82,000 in 1125 to 400,000 in 1207. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paper-money">Paper money</a> was first issued in Beijing during the Jin, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lugou-bridge">Lugou Bridge</a> was built in 1189.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadu">Dadu</a>, begun in 1267, was nearly twice the size of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhongdu">Zhongdu</a>. Its population was 418,000 in 1270 and 952,000 by 1327. Water came from the Jinshui and Gaoliang rivers and from channels built by the hydrologist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guo-shoujing">Guo Shoujing</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonghui-canal">Tonghui Canal</a>, completed in 1293, brought barges from Tongzhou to the lakes inside the city. The Ming built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forbidden-city">Forbidden City</a> and the Temple of Heaven between 1403 and 1420; the city had 960,000 residents in 1448, with 2.19 million in the surrounding region, and by the 15th century had essentially taken its current shape. Under the Qing it had 776,242 residents in 1781.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Beijing&#8221; cites where it covers Early and Mid-Imperial China:</p><ul><li>Stephen G. Haw, Beijing – A Concise History (Routledge, 2006)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kublai-khan.jpg" alt="Kublai Khan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YuanEmperorAlbumKhubilaiPortrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Araniko, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The state of Yan held Ji until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> took the city in 226 BCE; it became the seat of Guangyang <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">Commandery</a>, and under the Han the capital of the Kingdom of Yan and then of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/youzhou">Youzhou</a>. In 352 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-jun">Murong Jun</a> moved the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/former-yan">Former Yan</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> to Ji, making it a sovereign capital for the first time in over 500 years; five years later it moved to Ye. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> renamed the area Zhuo Commandery, and under the Tang it was Youzhou again, with a military commander who held it largely autonomously for 150 years after the rebellion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan">An Lushan</a> in 755.</p><p>In 936 the Later Jin ceded Youzhou to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Khitan Liao</a>, who made it Nanjing, the Southern Capital, in 938. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> Jin took it in 1122 as Yanjing; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> held it briefly in 1123 as Yanshan, and the Jin retook it in 1125. In 1153 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wanyan-liang">Wanyan Liang</a> moved the Jin capital there and named it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhongdu">Zhongdu</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> took the city in 1215, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadu">Dadu</a> built, also known by the Mongol name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanbaliq">Khanbaliq</a>, and in 1279, when the last of the Song fell, it became for the first time the capital of the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><p>In 1368 the Ming took Dadu and renamed it Beiping. Zhu Di, Prince of Yan, took the throne in 1402 as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a> and in 1403 renamed it Beijing, the Northern Capital. It became the main capital in 1421, and the Qing made it their seat in 1644. The Republic kept the capital in Beijing until 1928, when the Nationalists moved it to Nanjing and the city became Beiping again. Under Japanese occupation from 1937 it was Beijing once more, and in 1949 it became the capital of the People's Republic.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Beijing&#8221; cites where it covers Ming dynasty, Qing dynasty, Judiciary and procuracy:</p><ul><li>Lillian Li, Beijing: From Imperial Capital to Olympic City (Palgrave Macmillan, 2007)</li><li>Yi Wang, A Century of Change: Beijing's Urban Structure in the 20th Century (Springer, 2013)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/matteo-ricci"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matteo-ricci.jpg" alt="Matteo Ricci" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/matteo-ricci">Matteo Ricci</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:利玛窦像，游文辉绘.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chinese brother Emmanuel Pereira (born Yu Wen-hui 游文辉), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> kept the Tang plan of the city, with eight gates in the outer wall, an inner walled city converted into a palace complex, and 26 residential neighbourhoods. Khitan, Xi, Shiwei and Balhae people from the north and Han from the south settled in it. The Niujie Mosque dates from 996, the Fayuan Temple from 645 and the Tianning Temple from 1100 to 1119.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadu">Dadu</a> was laid out in a checkerboard of avenues 25 m wide and narrow lanes called hutongs, 6 to 7 m wide, with the main markets at Dongsi, Xisi and the north shore of Jishuitan. Builders came from across the Mongol empire, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>: the White Stupa Temple of 1271 was designed by the Nepali architect Araniko. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odoric-of-pordenone">Odoric of Pordenone</a> and Giovanni di Monte Corvino left accounts of visits, and the playwrights Ma Zhiyuan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-hanqing">Guan Hanqing</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-shifu">Wang Shifu</a> lived there.</p><p>Under the Ming the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matteo-ricci">Matteo Ricci</a> became an adviser to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wanli-emperor">Wanli Emperor</a> in 1601 and founded the Nantang Cathedral in 1605. Residents, who had burned wood, turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> as the forests around the city disappeared. The Qing assigned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eight-banners">Eight Banners</a> to live near the eight gates of the Inner City. In 1728 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongzheng-emperor">Yongzheng Emperor</a> ordered that candidates in the civil service examination speak Beijing Mandarin, and the Beijing dialect became the national standard for spoken Chinese in 1913. Peking Opera formed in the mid-19th century from Anhui and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubei">Hubei</a> troupes invited to court. Tongrentang opened in 1669, and Quanjude, which introduced the hung oven, in 1864.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Beijing&#8221; cites where it covers Architecture, Places of interest:</p><ul><li>Lillian Li, Beijing: From Imperial Capital to Olympic City (Palgrave Macmillan, 2007)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/chongzhen-emperor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chongzhen-emperor.jpg" alt="Chongzhen Emperor" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/chongzhen-emperor">Chongzhen Emperor</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chongzhen-Emperor.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 979 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> emperor Taizong besieged Nanjing and was defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gaoliang-river">Battle of Gaoliang River</a>. In 1214 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhongdu">Zhongdu</a> and withdrew after a treaty; he besieged it again, and in 1215, after many inhabitants had starved, its defenders surrendered and it was looted and burned. In 1449 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esen-taishi">Esen Tayisi</a> marched on the city with the captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhengtong-emperor">Zhengtong Emperor</a>, and was repelled. Epidemics in 1643 claimed 200,000 lives, and in March 1644 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-zicheng">Li Zicheng</a>'s forces took the city and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongzhen-emperor">Chongzhen Emperor</a> hanged himself.</p><p>In 1860 Anglo-French forces took the city, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-bruce-8th-earl-of-elgin">Lord Elgin</a> ordered the Old Summer Palace burned in retaliation for the mistreatment of Western prisoners. In June 1900 Qing forces and Boxers besieged the Legation Quarter, which sheltered several hundred foreigners and about 3,200 Chinese Christians; the Eight-Nation Alliance lifted the siege, looted the city and occupied it. The Japanese occupation from 1937 spared the city urban fighting, but a Japanese biological warfare unit, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china">North China</a> Unit 1855, released cholera in southern Beijing in 1943, killing 1,872 residents.</p><p>In 1958 and 1959 about 420,000 residents were sent to the countryside during the shortages of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-leap-forward">Great Leap Forward</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-guards-china">Red Guards</a> ransacked 114,000 homes in a month, and at least 1,772 residents were killed in August and September 1966. On 3 and 4 June 1989 troops fired on protesters in and around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiananmen">Tiananmen</a> Square; hundreds of civilians were killed, thousands wounded and thousands detained, and the government has barred reporting or remembrance of the events.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tiananmen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tiananmen.jpg" alt="Tiananmen" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tiananmen">Tiananmen</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20200110_Tiananmen-3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Balon Greyjoy, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Beijing has been <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s capital for most of the time since 1279, and the Beijing dialect is its standard speech: the Republic adopted it in 1913, the Nationalists confirmed it in 1932 and the People's Republic in 1955. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forbidden-city">Forbidden City</a>, opened to the public as the Palace Museum after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puyi">Puyi</a> was evicted in 1924, the Temple of Heaven, the Summer Palace, the Ming Tombs and the Zhoukoudian Peking Man Site are among eight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a> in the city.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peking-university">Peking University</a> was founded in 1898. The American Indemnity College, set up in 1911 with Boxer indemnity funds as a preparatory school, became Tsinghua University in 1912. On 4 May 1919 about 3,000 students gathered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiananmen">Tiananmen</a> Square against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>, a protest that began a tradition of student activism in the city. On 1 October 1949 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> proclaimed the People's Republic from Tiananmen. Tiananmen Square was expanded and the Great Hall of the People and the National Museum were completed by 1959; the Ming city walls were pulled down in the 1960s for the subway and the 2nd Ring Road.</p><p>The city has since spread outward along ring roads, lost many old hutong neighbourhoods and hosted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/2008-summer-olympics">2008 Summer Olympics</a>. It is the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>, with a municipal population of about 22 million.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/mo-li-hua">Mo Li Hua<span>1765</span></a><a href="/songs/the-internationale">The Internationale<span>1871</span></a><a href="/songs/march-of-the-volunteers">March of the Volunteers<span>1935</span></a><a href="/songs/the-east-is-red">The East Is Red<span>1942</span></a><a href="/songs/without-the-communist-party-there-would-be-no-new-china">Without the Communist Party, There Would Be No New China<span>c. 1943</span></a><a href="/songs/ode-to-the-motherland">Ode to the Motherland<span>1950</span></a><a href="/songs/sailing-the-seas-depends-on-the-helmsman">Sailing the Seas Depends on the Helmsman<span>1964</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Beijing began as the frontier city of Ji and grew into a capital under the Jin, Yuan, Ming and Qing, changing its name as its standing changed. It was the capital of the Republic until 1928 and has been the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> since 1949.</em></p>

